# KNOWN GAP (found R47 Track D1 while building the ACCEPT control for the
# primitive-receiver reject).
#
# An `equip int:` block in the program breaks the STATIC call `int.parse(...)`.
# The type NAME in receiver position is lowered as if `parse` were a user equip
# method on `int`, so both compilers emit a call to a mangling nothing defines:
#
#   RUST gg   : link error — `undefined reference to `int64_t__parse``
#   SELF-HOST : same
#
# BOTH LANES AGREE ON THE WRONG ANSWER (Core #8 red flag). Neither the `equip`
# block nor the static call is at fault on its own — remove either and the
# program builds and prints `99`. The two are independent language features and
# their combination has no ratified special case, so this is a defect, not a
# design.
#
# It is INDEPENDENT of R47 Track D1's reject: it reproduces identically on the
# pre-fix driver, and `reject_no_method_on_primitive` never sees a static call
# (the receiver is a type name, which resolves to no type at all).
#
# CORRECT behaviour: prints `99`.
equip int:
    int doubled(self):
        return self * 2

void main():
    if int.parse("99") is Some(n):
        print(n)
